LESSON 6: SELECTION

BRACKETS AFTER A VECTOR PICK ELEMENTS BY POSITION, COUNTING
FROM 1. THE INDEX MAY BE A VECTOR TOO. TRY:

      V←10 20 30 40
      V[3]
      V[1 3]
      V[4 3 2 1]

THE ARROW, ←, GIVES V ITS VALUE, AND V KEEPS IT UNTIL YOU
CHANGE IT. A LINE THAT ONLY ASSIGNS PRINTS NOTHING. AN
INDEXED NAME ON THE LEFT OF THE ARROW CHANGES ONE ELEMENT:
V[2]←25, AND THEN V.
(TRY IT. TYPE GO TO GO ON, OR STOP TO LEAVE.)
⎕:
      GO
↑ TAKES THE FIRST N ELEMENTS AND ↓ DROPS THEM; A NEGATIVE
N COUNTS FROM THE END. TRY:

      2↑V
      ¯1↑V
      1↓V
      ¯2↓V

⌽ REVERSES, AND N⌽V ROTATES BY N: 1⌽V MOVES THE FIRST
ELEMENT TO THE END.
(TRY IT. TYPE GO TO GO ON, OR STOP TO LEAVE.)
⎕:
      GO
A VECTOR OF 1S AND 0S ON THE LEFT OF / SELECTS: THIS IS
COMPRESSION, AND WITH A COMPARISON IT IS HOW APL FILTERS.
TRY:

      (V>15)/V
      1 0 1 1/V
      1 0 1 1\1 2 3

THE LAST IS EXPANSION: THE 0 OPENS A GAP, FILLED WITH 0.
(TRY IT. TYPE GO TO GO ON, OR STOP TO LEAVE.)
⎕:
      GO
∊ ASKS WHETHER EACH ELEMENT ON THE LEFT IS SOMEWHERE ON
THE RIGHT, AND V⍳X ASKS WHERE: THE POSITION OF X IN V, OR
ONE PAST THE END IF IT IS NOT THERE. TRY:

      30∊V
      V⍳30
      V⍳99

END OF LESSON 6. TYPE QUIZ 6 TO CHECK, OR NEXT TO GO ON.
